+import Accelerate
+import AVFoundation
+
+// Real-time onset detector fed by a mic input tap on the same AVAudioEngine
+// the metronome renders its click through (see MetronomeEngine's
+// startOnsetDetection). Replaces the app's previous JS pipeline, which only
+// ever saw a single broadband `metering` dB value polled every 20ms and
+// decimated into 50ms buckets — far too coarse to separate two hits closer
+// than ~80ms (e.g. consecutive sixteenth notes) or to reject the click
+// without blanking a big window around every beat.
+//
+// Technique: short-time spectral flux.
+//   - 512-sample Hann-windowed frames, hop 128 (~2.7ms at 48kHz), real FFT
+//     via vDSP.
+//   - Flux = sum of positive bin-to-bin magnitude increases, restricted to
+//     a ~200Hz–8kHz band (rumble/DC and the very top octave carry mostly
+//     noise, not attack energy — this, and only this, is where "ignore some
+//     frequencies" actually helps).
+//   - Adaptive threshold: rolling mean + k·stddev of recent flux.
+//   - A frame is an onset when its flux clears the threshold, is a local
+//     maximum, and sits at least `refractorySamples` past the previous
+//     onset (so a decaying first hit can't retrigger, while a genuine
+//     second hit ~60ms later still can).
+//
+// Click rejection is sample-accurate, not heuristic: MetronomeEngine knows
+// the exact output host-time of every click it renders and pushes a short
+// gate window (addClickGate) covering the click plus its speaker→mic
+// bleed. Frames inside a gate never emit an onset and don't update the
+// flux history, and the spectral difference is reseeded on the first frame
+// after the gate so the click's edge doesn't surface as a phantom onset.
+//
+// All onset/envelope timestamps are AVAudioTime host-time (ms), the same
+// clock MetronomeEngine now stamps every onBeat with — so JS can line an
+// onset up against beat 1 with no Date.now() dispatch jitter in the path.
+final class OnsetDetector {
+    // MARK: - Tunables
+
+    private static let fftSize = 512
+    private static let hopSize = 128
+    private static let log2n: vDSP_Length = 9  // 2^9 == fftSize
+
+    // Flux band, Hz. Below ~200Hz is handling/rumble and room boom; above
+    // ~8kHz is mostly hiss with little attack information. Widen the top if
+    // brushes/hi-hats read as too weak; raise the bottom if a boomy room
+    // still triggers.
+    private static let bandLoHz: Double = 200
+    private static let bandHiHz: Double = 8_000
+
+    // Rolling window (frames) the adaptive threshold's mean/stddev are
+    // computed over — ~250ms at the hop above. Long enough to be a stable
+    // noise-floor estimate, short enough to track a real crescendo.
+    private static let fluxHistoryFrames = 90
+
+    // Threshold = mean + thresholdK · stddev, floored at thresholdFloor
+    // (absolute, so a dead-silent room can't drive the threshold to ~0 and
+    // fire on nothing). Raise thresholdK if quiet background noise trips it;
+    // lower it if soft hits are missed.
+    private static let thresholdK: Float = 1.6
+    private static let thresholdFloor: Float = 0.004
+
+    // Minimum spacing between two onsets. 55ms still resolves four
